<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siraf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siraf was a port on the Iranian coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, near modern Bandar Taheri in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a> province. In the 9th and 10th centuries it was the Gulf's leading harbour, trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After an earthquake in 977 and political changes it declined, and its trade passed to the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>. Excavations in the 1960s and 1970s uncovered its mosque, houses and harbour.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siraf.jpg" alt="Siraf" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MrSamma · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siraf-Wharf.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The ruins of Siraf lie on the north shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, about 220 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a>. The town stood on a narrow strip between a shallow bay, 4 km long, and a sandstone ridge 500 m inland, and the old city stretched for 2 km along the shore west of a spur where the local shaikh had his fortified residence. Passes through the coastal ridges are rare; the Kunarak valley probably carried the road to the Jam plain then as now. Siraf had little farmland and, according to al-Maqdisi, only one small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qanat">qanat</a>, so food and water were brought from the Jam plain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations have uncovered a Sasanian port, which probably served the inland city of Gor (now Firuzabad), and a large castle possibly built about 360 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a>, while Indian red polished ware of the 5th and 6th centuries points to trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. In the 9th century the city was rebuilt and greatly expanded. Its congregational mosque, one of the earliest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, was built in two phases, the second around 850. About 900 the Sirafi merchant Abu Zayd Hasan wrote of Sirafi ships trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a> and Zanzibar.</p><p>Istakhri, shortly before 950, called Siraf the second city of the district of Ardashir-Khwarrah after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, listing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, sandalwood, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a>, ambergris and precious stones among its goods, and Ibn al-Balkhi put its annual trade in 908 to 932 at 2.53 million dinars. Merchants lived in multi-storey houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fired-brick">fired brick</a> and teak imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, one of which might cost 30,000 dinars. Al-Maqdisi traced its decline to the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, while Ibn al-Balkhi dated it to after their fall in 1055, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> from Qays Island attacked its ships.</p><p>Trade went on at a smaller scale. The merchant Abu'l-Qasim Ramisht (died 1140) ran a business from Siraf that reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the port, by then called Shilaw, remained a regional centre until the 15th or 16th century. In 1835 the British naval officer G.N. Kempthorne was the first to identify the ruins with Siraf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The modern town, long known as Taheri, became a city in 2005 and took back the name Bandar Siraf in 2008; it is the capital of Siraf District in Kangan County. More than 16,000 finds from the excavations of David Whitehouse and his team are kept in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, and empty rock-cut graves line the Shilaw valley behind the site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bandar Siraf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trade through the Persian Gulf, with <b>Siraf</b> as its leading port, linked the Buyid lands with India, East Africa and China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ships from Basra and <b>Siraf</b> sailed to India and China, and a whole section of the market of east Baghdad sold Chinese goods, from silk to porcelain; caravans brought spices, gold and slaves, and Jewish merchants known as the Radhanites linked the Muslim world with Christian Europe.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>As the port of <b>Siraf</b> declined after its 10th-century earthquake, merchants and sea captains on the island of Kish, called Qays in Arabic, took over its trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-kish">Emirate of Kish</a></li></ul></section>
